#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133536 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/id #uuid "251ec7c6-a050-5deb-8f13-d81f5de4b5c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5610" :question/problem "Find the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(X)\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(β) &= \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T$, we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and\n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X)^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nβ^(^) &= (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{104}\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6.75 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\ny = 6.75 - x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "a763a84a-0727-58a3-b524-04afa5735706" :question/math-academy-id "e-5611" :question/problem "An experiment consists of measuring how much a spring stretches (in inches) when one end of the spring is attached to the ceiling while a mass of $x$ ounces hangs from the other end. This data is shown below.\n\n| Mass $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $0.8$ | $1.8$ | $3.2$ | $4.2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the spring stretch if we use an $8$-ounce mass, assuming that the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(X)\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(β) &= \\begin{bmatrix}0.8 \\\\ 1.8 \\\\ 3.2 \\\\ 4.2\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T$, we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and\n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X)^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nβ^(^) &= (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 38.2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.04 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line\n\n$$\ny = 0.04+ 0.82x\n$$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the spring will stretch if an $8$-ounce mass is hung from it, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 0.04 + 0.82(8) \\\\\n&= 6.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, according to the model, if an $8$ ounce mass is hung on the spring, the spring will stretch $6.6$ inches.\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133641 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6acfe386-f7e4-54b1-9701-6ff5905b4536" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Regression Line in Terms of Matrices and Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "29b2a4d7-7e88-5f7a-9a84-ff5315c38f92" :question/math-academy-id "e-9914" :question/problem "The vector of coefficients of the line $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as\n\n| $x$ | **$-6$** | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Which are the design matrix $X$ and the observation vector $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}-6$** | **$\\color{blue}-3$** | **$\\color{blue}-1$** | **$\\color{blue}0$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}-7$ | $\\color{red}-4$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}8$ | $\\color{red}10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(X)\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(β) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6acfe386-f7e4-54b1-9701-6ff5905b4536" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Regression Line in Terms of Matrices and Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20594c2-8b13-5743-9418-00ca234bb530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130732} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c59f9d-b512-54e7-9cb1-7c7e2fe4dae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130733} {:answer/id #uuid "d83d477f-73fa-5372-b7a8-321ce9811cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130734} {:answer/id #uuid "f1374cf2-7b4c-577f-9f53-fda2e786227d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130735} {:answer/id #uuid "19f0bcaf-a2eb-5f63-bfcc-39ae986682d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d83d477f-73fa-5372-b7a8-321ce9811cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130734} :answer-field/id #uuid "47431566-66ec-59a7-a8b0-d58094c893d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130731}] :question/id #uuid "07c1a1e5-eac4-585d-8460-669cc1e39819" :question/math-academy-id "q-139517" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the line $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y$,\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa48db7d-42b9-542f-9132-5f9480ba31ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-1.7 - 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130744} {:answer/id #uuid "b293a154-6bc4-55d9-96c8-478e48edb3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 6 - 1.7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130745} {:answer/id #uuid "0a91356e-c0af-5bcb-8392-8c31bb66251a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-1.7 + 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130746} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1d51eb-c2c0-594e-8a70-852787541bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-6 + 1.7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} {:answer/id #uuid "54ac3134-61ee-573c-99be-ef16ece83c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1.7 - 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b293a154-6bc4-55d9-96c8-478e48edb3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 6 - 1.7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130745} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e530e9-f1e8-5bc9-9ded-9ed90868c02d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130743}] :question/id #uuid "72cc6e31-5631-5692-b09e-242241827364" :question/math-academy-id "q-139618" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$(X^{T}X)^{-1} = \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, X^{T}y = \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}$,\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "979c5af4-9964-538b-b144-532063ca3f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130762} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d790f1-d20b-5c8a-bbc1-23c707806914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130763} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf336cd-ac26-5998-9be3-a638605f8e3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130764} {:answer/id #uuid "64aa2687-8d50-568b-9155-b17f093ad1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130765} {:answer/id #uuid "7d17029a-45a5-5838-89f1-19c40f3f3fb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9d790f1-d20b-5c8a-bbc1-23c707806914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130763} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb67d2ca-15d7-5506-b1d1-9f75b40c28f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130761}] :question/id #uuid "8f426df0-1f38-5a2e-b701-5e427a0357ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-139750" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA biologist has recorded data on a pine tree's age (in years) and height (in feet). This data is shown below.\n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.2$ | $2.5$ | $3.6$ | $4.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the tree's height after $8$ years, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$(X^{T}X)^{-1} = \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, X^{T}y = \\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix}$,\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
